# Testing Guidelines

## Test Organization

### File Structure

- Place all tests in a directory named `test/`
- Create test modules that match the project architecture
- Name all test files as `test_<FILENAME>.py` (e.g., `test_ingest.py`)
- Group tests by functionality

### Test Framework

- Use pytest as the test runner (not unittest)
- Create a test configuration file
- Add test commands to pyproject.toml
- Set up CI/CD pipeline for automated testing

## Required Test Coverage

### Core Components

- Write tests for all functions in `ingest.py`
- Test all functions in `llm_semantic_splitter.py`
- Test functions in the transfer files (`transfert_pdf_txt.py` and `transfert_ppt_pdf.py`)

## Unit Testing

### LLM Component Testing

- Use mocked responses for LLM calls during testing
- Test the semantic splitter with small sample documents
- Verify JSON parsing logic with various response formats
- Test error handling for missing environment variables

### Vector Database Testing

- Use an in-memory Qdrant instance for testing
- Create fixture data for embedding tests
- Test search functionality with known vectors
- Verify collection creation and management

### Document Processing Testing

- Test PDF to text conversion with sample files
- Verify column detection with different layouts
- Test PowerPoint conversion with sample presentations
- Use small test files to keep tests fast

## Integration Testing

### End-to-End Testing

- Test the complete document ingestion pipeline
- Verify the chatbot can retrieve relevant information
- Test with real-world document examples
- Validate embedding quality with similarity tests

### Environment Setup

- Use separate test collections in Qdrant
- Create isolated Azure OpenAI test endpoints if possible
- Use environment variable prefixes for test configurations
- Clean up test data after test runs
